---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import Container from '../../components/Container.astro';
import SectionIntro from '../../components/SectionIntro.astro';
import Card from '../../components/Card.astro';
import PullQuote from '../../components/PullQuote.astro';
import FaqList from '../../components/FaqList.astro';
import FaqItem from '../../components/FaqItem.astro';
import SiteCta from '../../components/SiteCta.astro';
import EditorialPhoto from '../../components/EditorialPhoto.astro';
import { withBase } from '../../data/site';
import distantRoad from '../../assets/images/editorial/editorial-distant-road.png';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Legacy in Motion | She Who Remembers"
  description="A pathway for women asking what now: lived wisdom, contribution, and purpose that does not require fame, a platform, or a new identity to prove."
>
  <Hero
    heading="Legacy in Motion"
    lede="Becoming the Ancestor You Needed"
    surface="light"
    spacing="compact"
  >
    <p>
      At a certain point the central question changes. Not{' '}
      <em>What happened to me?</em>
      {' '}but{' '}
      <em>What have I learned that is worth carrying forward?</em>
    </p>
    <p>
      This doorway is for that turn: when inner work has already mattered, and life is asking
      for a more honest form of continuation.
    </p>
  </Hero>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="When achievement is no longer the question">
        <p>Success can complete a chapter and still leave a woman under-occupied at the soul level.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          You may have built the career, kept the family standing, survived the demanding years,
          or become the person others still call when something needs handling. The résumé is
          not empty. The restlessness is not ingratitude. It is often unused maturity looking
          for a truer assignment than more proving.
        </p>
        <p>
          Legacy in Motion is for the season after the climb, or beside it: identity after
          caregiving, life after a long professional intensity, creative hunger that will not
          accept another productivity lecture. Contribution here is not a new performance of
          worth. It is what becomes possible when you no longer need the work to justify you.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="photo-breath">
        <EditorialPhoto
          src={distantRoad}
          variant="road"
          alt="A dirt road stretches through dry grass toward distant hills in warm evening light."
        />
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What ends with you">
        <p>
          Some inheritances deserve a dignified ending. Naming them is not the same as
          prosecuting the people who handed them over.
        </p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Families teach silence. Workplaces reward over-functioning. Cultures assign women the
          unpaid emotional labor and then call it nature. You may recognize a role you were
          given before you could consent: the peacemaker, the achiever, the one who does not
          need much.
        </p>
        <p>
          Ending a pattern with you can be as unglamorous as telling the truth sooner, resting
          without apology, or refusing to pass along a belief that once kept you safe and now
          keeps you small. This is not a hunt for villains. It is stewardship. Someone had to
          be the place the repetition stopped.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What continues through you">
        <p>Legacy is not only interruption. It is also transmission.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          What you have learned about presence, about how to stay in a hard conversation, about
          making something that did not exist yesterday, about values that can survive contact
          with real life: these can move through mentorship, friendship, family culture, art,
          and the way you occupy a room.
        </p>
        <p>
          Help is not the same as a campaign to save anyone. What you carry forward can be
          quieter than a platform and still change the weather for one person who needed a
          different example.
        </p>
      </div>
      <PullQuote>
        The most honest continuation is often the one that never asked to be impressive.
      </PullQuote>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container>
      <SectionIntro heading="Living wisdom">
        <p>Information can be borrowed. Wisdom has to be inhabited.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="split">
        <Card>
          <h3>Information</h3>
          <p>
            What you can summarize, recommend, or repeat. Insight you have read, frameworks you
            can name, advice that still sounds right in someone else's voice. Useful. Incomplete
            until it has been walked.
          </p>
        </Card>
        <Card variant="blush">
          <h3>Lived wisdom</h3>
          <p>
            What remains after you have paid for the lesson in time, error, repair, and
            unremarkable hours. It tends to be specific, humble, and strangely practical. It
            does not need to announce itself as teaching in order to teach.
          </p>
        </Card>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="Creation and mentorship">
        <p>There is more than one honest shape for a life that has become conscious.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="themes">
        <li>Art, writing, and making that is not a branding exercise</li>
        <li>A business that carries values without turning you into a mascot</li>
        <li>Family culture: what is spoken, what is no longer required</li>
        <li>Mentorship that returns people to themselves</li>
        <li>Friendship as a serious form of influence</li>
        <li>Teaching, gathering, and community that does not demand devotion</li>
        <li>Ordinary acts of courage that never become content</li>
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="Legacy without fame">
        <p>Visibility is a tool some women use. It is not the proof of a life that mattered.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          You do not need followers, a public platform, children, awards, or a leadership title
          in order to leave something true behind. A private life can still interrupt a pattern,
          hand someone a better sentence, or make a piece of work that outlives the mood in
          which it was made.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you do want a more public form, this pathway will not confuse that desire with
          destiny, or treat shyness as a spiritual failure. Scale is optional. Integrity is not.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="Who it fits">
        <p>Less a demographic than a posture toward what comes next.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="fit">
        <li>You have already done meaningful inner work, and you can feel a next question forming.</li>
        <li>You want contribution that does not require you to erase yourself in order to be useful.</li>
        <li>You want lived experience to become something someone else can actually use.</li>
        <li>You are willing to let purpose be specific, even if it never looks impressive from a distance.</li>
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="What this work is not">
        <p>No pressure campaign disguised as calling.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <ul class="not">
        <li>It is not a requirement to build a personal brand, or to become public.</li>
        <li>It is not a mandate to mentor, teach, or take responsibility for other women's lives.</li>
        <li>It is not pressure to forgive a family, or to rewrite harm as a gift.</li>
        <li>It is not a script to become a mother, a grandmother, or an archetypal elder on cue.</li>
        <li>It will not treat your next chapter as a product launch.</li>
      </ul>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="How this work is offered">
        <p>Forms stay flexible until an offering is actually open.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>
          Legacy in Motion may appear as a seminar, an advanced group journey, or private legacy
          work. There is no public calendar or fee listed here yet. When a container is ready,
          the details will be named plainly. Until then, a fit conversation is the right door.
        </p>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <Section surface="light" spacing="compact">
    <Container width="narrow">
      <SectionIntro heading="Questions">
        <p>The practical doubts this doorway tends to raise.</p>
      </SectionIntro>
      <FaqList>
        <FaqItem question="Do I need to be over 40?">
          <p>
            No. The work is organized around a stage of inquiry, not a birthday. It may
            resonate especially in maturity, but the central question is whether your attention
            is shifting toward contribution, continuation, and what comes next.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Does legacy mean children?">
          <p>
            No. Children are one possible form of continuation, not the definition of legacy.
            Legacy can move through friendship, art, work, mentorship, community, values,
            relationships, or private choices.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Do I need a public platform?">
          <p>
            No. Visibility is optional. A private life can still transmit wisdom, interrupt
            patterns, influence people, and create something worth carrying forward.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Is this for business owners?">
          <p>
            It can be, but it is not a business program. A business may be one place lived
            wisdom takes form. So can family culture, writing, art, mentorship, community,
            friendship, or something entirely private.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="What if I still have healing work to do?">
          <p>
            That does not disqualify you. Legacy does not require perfect completion. The
            question is whether contribution can begin without using it to avoid unfinished
            personal work.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
        <FaqItem question="Can legacy be private?">
          <p>
            Yes. Some of the most consequential forms of legacy are never branded, monetized,
            posted, or publicly recognized.
          </p>
        </FaqItem>
      </FaqList>
    </Container>
  </Section>

  <SiteCta
    heading="If the question has become what now"
    primary={{ label: 'Book a Fit Conversation', href: withBase('/book-a-conversation') }}
    secondary={{ label: 'Explore the Work', href: withBase('/the-work') }}
  >
    <p>
      Bring the restlessness that is not a complaint. We will see whether Legacy in Motion is
      the right room, or whether another part of the path still wants your attention.
    </p>
  </SiteCta>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .prose {
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .photo-breath {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .fit,
  .themes,
  .not {
    margin: 0;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }

  .fit li,
  .themes li,
  .not li {
    position: relative;
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-sm);
    padding-left: 1.15rem;
  }

  .fit li:last-child,
  .themes li:last-child,
  .not li:last-child {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .fit li::before,
  .themes li::before,
  .not li::before {
    content: "";
    position: absolute;
    left: 0;
    top: 0.7em;
    width: 0.4rem;
    height: 0.4rem;
    border: var(--border-width) solid var(--border-accent);
    border-radius: 50%;
  }

  .split {
    display: grid;
    gap: var(--space-lg);
  }

  .split h3 {
    margin-bottom: var(--space-sm);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .split {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, minmax(0, 1fr));
    }
  }
</style>
